.TH TOOLBAR 1
.SH NAME
toolbar \- window manager toolbar
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/toolbar
[
.B -s
] [
.B -p
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Toolbar
is designed to be run as the controlling application under
an instance of
.IR wm (1).
It runs an initialisation shell script,
provides a menu allowing the user to start new programs.
and shows icons representing windows that have been hidden.
.PP
When
.I toolbar
is started, it configures itself by means of the
.B /lib/wmsetup
shell script.
.I Toolbar
loads the shell
.IR sh (1),
and defines the following
shell built-in
commands before executing the script:
.HP
.B menu
.I title1
.RI [ title2]
.I command
.br
Insert an item at the top of the start menu.
.I Title1
is the text of the item on the main menu.
If
.I title2
is given then
.I title1
is a sub-menu with
.I title2
as the menu item.
.I Command
is executed by the shell whenever the item is selected.
An item with an empty command is displayed as a separator.
.HP
.B delmenu
.RI [ title1
.RI [ title2 ]]
.br
With no arguments, forget all menu items.
Given
.I title1
alone, remove the top-level item or entire sub-menu of that name;
given
.I title1
and
.I title2,
remove just that one sub-menu item.
.PP
The start menu is data-driven: every entry created by these built-ins is held
in an in-memory model, and the menu widget is rebuilt from it.
The same model is exposed for editing at run time in two ways.
.PP
.I Toolbar
serves the control file
.B /chan/wmmenu.
Writing a
.B menu
or
.B delmenu
line to it (the same syntax accepted in the script) adds or removes an entry
immediately, so any program can register itself in the start menu when it
starts and unregister when it exits; for example
.EX
	echo 'menu Games Tetris {wmrun wm/tetris}' >/chan/wmmenu
	echo 'delmenu Games Tetris'                >/chan/wmmenu
.EE
Only the
.B menu
and
.B delmenu
verbs are honoured.
Reading
.B /chan/wmmenu
returns the current menu as a script that recreates it.
.PP
The start menu also offers a
.B "Customize menu..."
item that opens a graphical editor for adding, removing and reordering
entries.
Changes take effect immediately; its
.B Save
button writes the menu as a script to
.BI /usr/ username /lib/menu ,
which
.B /lib/wmsetup
replays at the next login (it begins with a
.B delmenu,
so when present it fully defines the menu).
.PP
The standard
.B /lib/wmsetup
script executes the script
.BI /usr/ username /lib/wmsetup ,
enabling each user to have their own window manager configuration,
and then
.BI /usr/ username /lib/menu
if it exists.
.PP
Normally
.I toolbar
packs a menu button referring to the start menu at the left hand side of the tool bar.
The
.B -s
option suppresses that.
.PP
.I Toolbar
serves the shared
.I "snarf buffer"
used by cut and paste in
.IR wm (1)
applications,
except in hosted Inferno where the host's standard clipboard system is used instead,
via
.IR snarf (3).
If
.I toolbar
cannot find
.B /chan/snarf
or the
.B -p
option is given,
.I toolbar
will create its own snarf buffer, private to the set of
applications running under the current instance of
.IR wm (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/wmsetup
Initialisation shell-script.
.TP
.BI /usr/ username /lib/menu
Per-user start menu saved by the graphical editor; replayed at login.
.TP
.B /chan/wmmenu
Control file for run-time menu registration.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/toolbar.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR wm(1),
.IR tkclient (2),
.IR wmclient (2),
.IR toolbar (1),
.IR logon (1).
